# Privilege Question Concerning Reports in *Swadesamitran*, *Navamani*, *Navasakthi* and *Jayaperikai*
Tamil title: சுதேசமித்திரன், நவமணி, நவசக்தி, ஜெயபேரிகை நாளிதழ்களில் வந்துள்ள செய்திபற்றி உரிமைப் பிரச்சினை

Date: 31 August 1968.

The source introduces this as Perarignar Anna's final address in the Legislative Assembly. It says the statesman who guided his younger brothers and the world with the threefold maxim “Duty, Dignity and Discipline” laments conduct lacking those qualities, reiterates them in his final political speech, and does not hate those who take another path and revile him: “Let us forget and forgive” is his consolation.

**Hon. C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Speaker, three privilege questions have been raised here over the same matter. The member who raised the second of them, the Hon. Mr Aranganathan, referred to statements by Tamil Nadu Congress Committee President C. Subramaniam and former Tamil Nadu legislator Mrs Ananthanayaki. Both spoke on the basis of reports in *Navamani* and other newspapers. Therefore, I consider the issue to be the publication of an incorrect newspaper report. Neither the speech of the Tamil Nadu Congress Committee President nor that of Mrs Ananthanayaki need be included in the privilege question.

The Tamil Nadu Congress Committee President clearly said, “If he spoke in that manner, it must be expunged from the Assembly record.” Even if I had inadvertently said such a thing, I myself would certainly have told you it should be removed from the proceedings. What happened, however, is that *Navamani* and *Swadesamitran* published something I did not say, and *Swadesamitran* went further and wrote an editorial about it.

Before I spoke here, our friend Mr Ramamurthi raised a “one-week question.” Even if one takes a week, there is an opportunity to correct this. What I said could also have been corrected then. What I have here is uncorrected; it reproduces what was spoken exactly.

[Interruption.]

What I said was this: “I heard that a woman speaker in Congress did not merely say that women could not walk in the streets, but slapped her thigh and issued a challenge, ‘Let anyone come if there is anyone.’ Tamil Nadu continues to give them room. How will the people of Tenkasi tolerate your saying that women cannot move about? I said I had heard it. Do not ask whether I witnessed it. I am not in a condition to watch that dreadful spectacle. I have written many stories and read many stories, but I have never written about a woman slapping her thigh, nor have I read of one in any story. Hers may be a strong thigh!” That is what I said.

I said, “At this moment I wish only to point out that the people of the country have not accepted your assertion that women cannot move about.” They said, and continue to say, that women cannot move about or go outside in this country. I spoke in response to that.

They speak even about such matters. I said it only to point out that you had not accepted their statement.

After *Navamani* published its report and *Swadesamitran* wrote its editorial, they wrote under the impression that I had referred to the women of Tamil Nadu as “cheap goods.” I may speak very sharply, but even at such moments I have never intended to speak without dignity. Nor did such an intention arise that day.

If I were to speak degradingly about the women of Tamil Nadu, it would affect my mother, my sister and every woman in my family. No such undignified phrase came from my mouth. The false report that I used the expressions “cheap goods” or “goods that would not sell” in this context properly falls within a question of privilege.

I read out my actual words because our friend the Leader of the Opposition asked that they be read. I did not degradingly describe women as “cheap goods.” Indeed, the idea implicit in my speech was that although Tamil Nadu Congress permits people to speak in this manner, the motherhood of Tamil Nadu possesses dignity and worth, and no one should conduct herself in a way that diminishes it. Not even inadvertently, at any moment, have I thought of insulting the women of Tamil Nadu or speaking of them in a degrading manner.

But elsewhere in this debate I did use the phrase “goods that would not sell.” When reporters wrote it down for publication, they did not apply it to the subject about which I spoke, but applied it here instead.

What I said was that, like merchants who, upon seeing evening darkness approach, loudly hawk unsaleable goods, drive away the flies and mosquitoes clinging to them, and take them home from the market, you shouted these wares for sale street by street. You told stories from platforms, repeated them at conferences and raised slogans in processions. What verdict did the public successively deliver after hearing all this?

During the South Madras parliamentary election, you told the public these same things and every related allegation. You said the public had then emerged from its intoxication. Whether or not they truly believed the people would vote only for Congress, that is what they said outside. But I asked what actually happened, and described the allegations Congress generally circulates against us as “goods that would not sell.” I did not even call them “cheap goods.” Had I said “cheap goods,” your case would have possessed some force. I clearly said “goods that would not sell.”

Reporters or newspaper proprietors took that expression, changed it to “cheap goods,” placed it where I had not used it, and then attempted to advise me to behave with dignity. If any newspaper proprietor comes forward to advise me, I am prepared to welcome it. They have both the right and the duty. But inventing a phrase and writing as though I had insulted womanhood is not advice. It is they who thereby insult womanhood. Publishing in their newspaper a sentence I did not speak, and another newspaper writing criticism upon it—both actions fall within the question of privilege, however strong the evidence they may claim.

Even now I wish to make a request concerning them. Give those newspapers an opportunity today. If they use it to recognise that their report saying I used “cheap goods” was wrong and publish that “Annadurai did not utter in his speech that expression insulting womanhood,” the matter may thereafter be dropped. If, on the other hand, they persist and repeat it, referring the matter to privilege at that point would itself demonstrate the dignity we owe the press.

For my part, anyone who believes newspapers should be conducted with dignity will accept this. I expect them to publish the clarification. I called neither the women of Tamil Nadu nor the Congress women speakers “cheap goods.” I called all those Congress allegations “goods that would not sell.” I have never possessed, do not now possess and did not then possess the base character required to speak that way about those women speakers or the women of Tamil Nadu.

Therefore, I think it sufficient to advise the newspapers to publish a proper correction while giving them an opportunity to do so.

[Interruption.]

**Hon. C. N. Annadurai:** The question now raised is that some newspapers published something I had not said, and others wrote criticism based upon it. I said that if those newspapers publish my explanation and express regret, there will be no occasion to refer it as a privilege question. If the Leader of the Opposition believes that parts of my own speech require correction, he may raise them on another occasion and they will be considered. If he brings them into this matter, however, they have no connection with this privilege question.

[A passage recording another intervention is illegible in the OCR.]

The Chief Minister is merely relating here what he heard. It is not a case of his reading out something published in a newspaper. He speaks on the basis of words he heard. Did the person who told him report them correctly, or did he hear them correctly?

[Interruption; part of the record is illegible in OCR.]

**Hon. C. N. Annadurai:** You are raising a different matter. I gave you an opportunity to raise it. That is why I said that, even on that very day, if any Congress member had risen and said, “No one in our party spoke or behaved that way,” I would immediately have discontinued that portion of my speech.

Friends who directly participated in the election are present here. If they say, “We know well that nothing of the kind happened,” I will show no hesitation at all in asking for its removal. Even there I spoke with great care. I said only, “I heard”; I did not say, “I saw it there.” At that very moment I expected one of you to stand and say it had not happened. I truly expected it.

[Interruption.]

Our Mr Vinayagam is speaking while remaining seated.

**Mr K. Vinayagam:** They refuse to give me an opportunity to stand and speak.

**Hon. C. N. Annadurai:** That is precisely why I am saying this, so that it enters the Assembly record. They say, “We deliberately allowed them to make the mistake.” It was the Opposition's duty to expose it. If we engage in a wrongful act or make an incorrect statement, the Opposition does not exist to refrain from pointing it out immediately and allow us to commit every error. That would itself be a failure of the Opposition's duty.

I do not believe they spoke with such an intention, because I was looking at them as I spoke. Many appeared distressed that such things had occurred; no one appeared to think they had not happened. I believe our Leader of the Opposition, Mr Karuthiruman, also campaigned there during the final two or three days of the election. If he or others who remained there say, “Nothing of that kind happened,” I will not hesitate to declare, “What I said was wrong, and I regret it.”

In this House, whenever I have made an error or spoken wrongly, I have never hesitated to withdraw it. I heard that during the Sankagiri election the Leader of the Opposition said, “Annadurai apologised a thousand times.” The error lies in failing to recognise a mistake. Once it is recognised, correcting it is the proper course.

[Interruption.]

Mr Vinayagam himself has agreed. Therefore, had the error been pointed out, I would have accepted it on that day itself. Investigate and tell me that the woman speaker did not behave in that way. You may investigate and tell me even afterward. I am prepared even then to say, “Very well, you say so, and therefore I accept it.” But it has no connection to the privilege question now before us.

**Mr K. Vinayagam:** A Congress public meeting took place yesterday evening. I, Mr C. S. and Mrs Ananthanayaki spoke at it. Mr C. S. said, “That election campaign was conducted under my leadership. I kept watch over it, and no speaker, to my knowledge, spoke while slapping her thigh in that way.” The Chief Minister has said that someone did so. Someone may have given him that information. The Chief Minister says an error must be corrected when it is discovered. I now state officially that Mr C. S. led and conducted the election. He observed how the speakers spoke and has said that, to the best of his knowledge, no one behaved that way. Will the Chief Minister therefore correct what he said?

**Hon. C. N. Annadurai:** It is true that Mr C. S. stood and conducted the Tenkasi election campaign on a full scale. But it cannot be true that he attended every meeting. Such a thing may have happened and such words may have been spoken at meetings he did not attend.

Our friend Mr Vinayagam says he heard Mr C. S. speak yesterday. I believe Mr C. S. and I are to meet on the sixth regarding another matter. If he tells me about this directly then, I shall certainly consider it.

The argument I presented now is simply that it was improper for some newspapers to publish something I did not say and for another newspaper to criticise me on that basis. That is also the argument of the members who brought the privilege question. I am surprised that the Leader of the Opposition has expressed no view on it.

The question is whether it is right or wrong for one newspaper to publish something as my statement when I did not say it and for another to criticise it on that basis. If the Leader of the Opposition joins me in declaring that those newspapers acted improperly and says they may be given an opportunity to publish a correction and express regret, he will have properly discharged his duty as Leader of the Opposition.

Just as the other newspapers are to be given an opportunity, this newspaper too should be given one. It should be allowed to publish a correction and express regret for writing in a manner that diminishes the dignity of this House by alleging that members have spoken without restraint in the Legislature for several days. I wish to say that this is the proper course for the Speaker to adopt.

---NOTES---
The retained source contains severe OCR damage in portions of pages 4 and 6, including fragments that appear to represent English-language interventions in the Assembly record. The translation marks those passages as illegible instead of inventing missing speech.

The distinction between “மலிவான சரக்கு” (cheap goods) and “விலை போகாத சரக்கு” (goods that would not sell / unsaleable goods) is central. Anna denies using either expression about women and states that the latter referred only to Congress allegations.

“உரிமைப் பிரச்சினை” is rendered as “privilege question,” the parliamentary procedure concerning an alleged breach of the privileges of the House or its members.

The source introduction identifies this as Anna's final Legislative Assembly speech and frames it through his maxim “கடமை - கண்ணியம் - கட்டுப்பாடு” (Duty, Dignity and Discipline).
